MariaDB @VERSION@ for OpenVMS x86-64 (vms-mariadb), kit @KITVERSION@
========================================================================

A native port of the MariaDB @VERSION@ server and command-line clients,
built with VSI's clang-based compiler (LP64).  This kit is a PREVIEW: read
"Limitations" before trusting data to it.

Product VMSMARIADB, PCSI version @PCSIVERSION@, producer @PRODUCER@.  It
installs in [VMSMARIADB] with the logical name VMSMARIADB$ROOT, so it does not
collide with VSI's own MariaDB kit.

Requirements
------------

- OpenVMS x86-64 V9.2-x or later (built and tested on E9.2-4).
- VSI's SSL3 kit (OpenSSL 3.0): the images use SYS$SHARE:SSL3$LIBSSL_SHR and
  SSL3$LIBCRYPTO_SHR.  PCRE2 and zlib are linked in.
- An ODS-5 disk for data directories.
- For a server: a reasonable page file quota (PGFLQUOTA; a server with the
  default buffers uses about 500 MB of virtual memory) and FILLM of 150 or
  more.  The server runs with the UAF quotas of the account that starts it.

Installing
----------

    $ PRODUCT INSTALL VMSMARIADB /SOURCE=dev:[dir]

then, as the post-installation text says:

- add  $ @SYS$STARTUP:VMSMARIADB$STARTUP.COM  to SYS$MANAGER:SYSTARTUP_VMS.COM
  (it defines VMSMARIADB$ROOT; it does not start a server);
- add  $ @VMSMARIADB$ROOT:[000000]VMSMARIADB$SETUP.COM  to LOGIN.COM (or
  SYLOGIN.COM) for the client commands.

PRODUCT REMOVE VMSMARIADB removes the product and VMSMARIADB$ROOT; it does not
touch data directories.

Contents
--------

    VMSMARIADB$ROOT:[BIN]      MARIADBD.EXE (the server), MARIADB.EXE (the
                               client), MARIADB-ADMIN, -CHECK, -DUMP, -IMPORT,
                               -SHOW, -SLAP, MY_PRINT_DEFAULTS, PERROR
    VMSMARIADB$ROOT:[SHARE]    error messages (one directory per language)
                               and [.CHARSETS]
    VMSMARIADB$ROOT:[SCRIPTS]  the SQL that creates the system tables
    VMSMARIADB$ROOT:[000000]   VMSMARIADB$SETUP.COM, VMSMARIADB$SERVER.COM
    VMSMARIADB$ROOT:[DOC]      this file, COPYING (GPL v2), CREDITS

Client commands
---------------

VMSMARIADB$SETUP.COM defines MARIADB, MARIADB_ADMIN, MARIADB_CHECK,
MARIADB_DUMP, MARIADB_IMPORT, MARIADB_SHOW, MARIADB_SLAP, MY_PRINT_DEFAULTS,
PERROR and MARIADB_SERVER.  Options are case-sensitive (-P is the port, -p
the password), so either SET PROCESS/PARSE_STYLE=EXTENDED or quote them:

    $ mariadb "-h" dbhost "-P" 3306 "-u" me "-p" mydb
    $ mariadb_dump "--host=dbhost" "--user=me" "-p" mydb > mydb.sql

The clients connect over TCP only: give -h (127.0.0.1 for a local server);
"localhost" means a Unix-domain socket elsewhere, which VMS lacks.  TLS works
(OpenSSL 3.0 from the SSL3 kit).

Running a server
----------------

    $ @VMSMARIADB$ROOT:[000000]VMSMARIADB$SERVER INSTALL_DB DKA100:[MARIADB.DATA]
    $ @VMSMARIADB$ROOT:[000000]VMSMARIADB$SERVER START DKA100:[MARIADB.DATA]
    $ mariadb "-h" 127.0.0.1 "-u" root
    MariaDB [(none)]> ALTER USER root@localhost IDENTIFIED BY 'secret';
    $ @VMSMARIADB$ROOT:[000000]VMSMARIADB$SERVER STOP 3306 "--password=secret"

- INSTALL_DB creates the data directory and, beside it, <name>_TMP (the
  server's temporary directory), both with /VERSION_LIMIT=1: MariaDB replaces
  files by truncation and rename, which on VMS would otherwise leave old
  versions behind.  It creates the system tables; the root accounts
  (root@localhost, root@127.0.0.1, ...) have no password: set one at once.
- START runs the server as a detached process MARIADBD_<port> (default port
  3306) under the starting user's UAF quotas.  Output goes to
  <datadir>VMSMARIADB$SERVER.LOG, the error log to <datadir>mariadbd.err.
  The account owns the data files: use a dedicated account for a real server.
- Options: if <datadir>MY.CNF exists it is the server's only option file;
  otherwise the server runs with --no-defaults.  START's fourth parameter
  passes one more option, e.g. "--bind-address=127.0.0.1".
- STOP asks the server to shut down (mariadb-admin as root); STATUS reports
  it.  Always stop the server this way: STOP/ID loses unflushed data.

Limitations of this preview
---------------------------

- Storage engines: MyISAM (the default), Aria, MEMORY, CSV, MRG_MyISAM and
  SEQUENCE.  No InnoDB (and so no transactions), no Performance Schema, no
  replication testing, no plugins loaded at run time.
- TCP only (no Unix-domain socket); no unix_socket authentication.
- Uncached reads are slow: the C RTL issues an extra disk I/O for every
  read call, so full scans of MyISAM tables with dynamic rows (CHECKSUM
  TABLE, CHECK TABLE) run at a few hundred kilobytes per second.
- A 24-hour soak test has not been run yet.
- The server sizes its table cache by open_files_limit, not by the process's
  FILLM quota: with many tables, raise FILLM or set table_open_cache in MY.CNF.
- Tested: system-table creation, a client suite over TCP and TLS, all six
  engines, 1,000,000-row (150 MB) Aria and MyISAM loads with five clean
  stop/start cycles (row counts, checksums and CHECK TABLE identical), and
  remote clients from Linux.

